  "summary": "A Los Gatos resident op-ed argues the Town lacks binding ordinances and oversight for Flock ALPR cameras, citing a camera installed on a utility pole without clear authorization under a Monte Sereno contract that placed cameras inside Los Gatos. The author proposes a 10-point 'Bill of Rights' for automated surveillance covering access limits, retention, sharing, signage, and binding ordinances.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Monte Sereno approved a contract with Flock Safety for up to 19 cameras, including several inside Los Gatos to form a security perimeter.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "Santa Cruz terminated its Flock contract, while Santa Clara County prohibited the Sheriff's Office from using Flock as a vendor because of concerns about data security and information sharing.",
      "paragraph_idx": 12
    },
    {
      "quote": "Pass binding municipal ordinances rather than relying on internal departmental policies.",
      "paragraph_idx": 14
    }
  ],
